19 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428294 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428295 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Definite Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1033 1685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1034 1.4972104418513188 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050819 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "68fac9fb-a6fb-5c4b-a510-881f3f9dcbd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084560 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "72cd6e5a-2562-5b0e-a9ea-4e4dbfbc3c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084728 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "82032938-ca92-55b7-b0fb-f0c89679a907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087639 #edb/ref 1069 "e-765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087639 #edb/ref 1071 "Starting with the standard Maclaurin result for $e^x,$ find the first three terms of the series expansion for $\\displaystyle{\\int e^{-x^2}\\textrm{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16b3f250-dfcb-5fa6-bfdb-4acf92d8bd37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087639 #edb/ref 1074 "The series expansion of $e^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =1+(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087640 #edb/ref 1069 "e-766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087640 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\ln(1+x)$, find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1/2}\\ln(1-x^2)\\text{d}x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4211b707-bff1-5855-9449-16e14858e6ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087640 #edb/ref 1074 "The power series expansion of $\\ln(1+x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}) & =(-{x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(-{x}^{2})^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+⋯){|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5\\cdot {2}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7\\cdot {2}^{7}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}-\\frac{1}{320}-\\frac{1}{2688}+⋯\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can get a numerical approximation by evaluating the first three terms. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ≈-\\frac{1}{24}-\\frac{1}{320}-\\frac{1}{2688}=-0.04516\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to five decimal places.\n\nThe exact value of the integral, rounded to five decimal places, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =-0.04523\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our estimation was pretty good!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186087970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186087999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Indefinite Integrals Using Taylor Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087793 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eea68c90-99a8-5254-b799-5f0ed51615a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186087971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Definite Integrals Using Taylor Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087794 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1f133583-cb11-5007-bdef-641838aff5e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1045 "Approximating Integrals Using Taylor Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087951 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b3fd6ef1-04ad-5c0a-b327-86d38cf19448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1-x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1-2{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f631e970-6542-5e8c-b936-21da7739cd3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087971 #edb/ref 1074 "The power series expansion of $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2{x}^{3}$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-2{x}^{3}}=1+2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{6}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1-2{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1+2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{6}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7}+…){|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{1}{224}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087999 #edb/ref 1071 "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for ${e}^{x},$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for\n$\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccde4599-faf3-5f96-becc-243584d2e9c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087999 #edb/ref 1074 "The series expansion of ${e}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-{x}^{2}$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =1+(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}(-{x}^{2})^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the series by $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =x\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯) \\\\  & =x-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}+⋯)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =C+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088678 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088678 #edb/ref 1061 2600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ceb1bdb-03e5-5b90-9089-ebb348602688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088679 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088679 #edb/ref 1061 2601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3701ede4-42d5-5b06-ae18-a15fcdbae21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088680 #edb/ref 1061 2604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3e25ee3-5f82-5928-a669-03387e398d24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088853 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos \\sqrt{x} \\, \\textrm dx\\,\n$$\n\nUnfortunately, we cannot compute this integral using any of our standard methods. However, we *can* express this integral as an infinite series if we first write the integrand $\\cos \\sqrt{x}$ as a Maclaurin series.\n\nLet's start with the Maclaurin series expansion of $\\cos{x},$ valid for all $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}\t=1 - \\frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \\frac{1}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\,.\n$$\n\n    \n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above equation, we find the series expansion for $\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\sqrt{x})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}(\\sqrt{x})^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nIntegrating both sides of this equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,dx & =\\int (1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{72}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nAnd we're done! We've just found the Maclaurin series expression for $\\displaystyle \\int\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$\n\nWe can apply the same method to any function $f(x)$ having a power series expansion of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=a_0+a_1 x+a_2 x^2 + \\cdots\\,.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({a}_{0}+{a}_{1}x+{a}_{2}{x}^{2}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={a}_{0}\\int \\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{1}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+{a}_{0}x+{a}_{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is valid for the same radius of convergence as the series expansion of $f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088853 #edb/ref 1065 1322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088853 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b0a3a223-98fa-56e3-b1df-6cb6d1bcdaf1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088853 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531946 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1+\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{80}+⋯" #edb/ref 17592186045417